                    },
                    "message": "Sorry i missed the review here.  Is this still desired?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Paul Fertser",
                        "email": "fercerpav@gmail.com",
                        "username": "paulfertser"
                    },
                    "message": "> Sorry i missed the review here.  Is this still desired?\n\nYes, the affected code wasn't modified since then so it's still needed if one wants to have PECI-provided thresholds for the temperature. If this feature is not needed at all it should be removed altogether as it's currently broken without this patch.\n\nPlease keep in mind that PECI is an unreliable transport especially so during host bootup (also during host relatively deep states)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/Thresholds.cpp",
                    "line": 521,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "This throws an exception possibly the way you've written it which is uncaught.  please use the version that takes an error_code"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/Thresholds.cpp",
                    "line": 521,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Paul Fertser",
                        "email": "fercerpav@gmail.com",
                        "username": "paulfertser"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes, it can throw an exception in case the OS returns some really unexpected filesystem-level error or similar (\"underlying OS API errors\"). If I am to handle it, what would be the action to take? I'd say it still warrants termination of the proocess, and default action on uncaught exception is exactly that, it also prints `what()` automatically so provides useful diagnostics.\n\nPlease note that \"No filesystem exception is thrown if object does not exist (use return value).\" I wrote and tested this code snippet, it was necessary for the changes made to PECI temperature daemon here. It didn't throw any exceptions (why would it for sysfs ever?)."
                }
            ],
